@meta
id: 06-08
title: Drill — f to the colon
chapter: 06
type: drill
skills: find-f, line-start
par: 56
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: four double-clicks and four question marks, eight times
open: order.record.ts

@brief
Four fields, all of them becoming optional. Each line needs a `?` inserted
immediately before its colon. Eight passes over the file.

The rep is `^` to the start of the code, `f:` onto the colon, `i?` `Esc`,
`j` down.

@keys
| `^` | first non-blank |
| `f:` | forward to the colon |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
Press `^` at the start of every line. Without it you are counting from
wherever the last insert dropped you, and on a shorter line there may be no
colon left ahead of you.
--
Do not read the field names. `f:` does not care what they are called, and
neither should you.

@solution
    four times: j ^ f : i ? <Esc>

Six keys a field, and none of them depend on how long the field name is.
That independence is the thing to notice — the same rep works on `id` and on
`cancellationReason` at identical cost, which is why `f` scales to real code
where names are long and columns are meaningless.

The `^` at the head of each line is a habit worth keeping for life. Motions
like `f` measure from the cursor, so a rep that does not start from a known
position is a rep that eventually lands somewhere surprising. Start from an
anchor, travel by name, insert, move on.

@start order.record.ts
export type OrderRecord = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  cancellationReason: string;
};

@expected order.record.ts
export type OrderRecord = {
  id?: string;
  customerId?: string;
  status?: OrderStatus;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};
